Date: Sat, 09 Oct 93 08:04:38 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: Meaninglessness

In my discussion with Dave Burns about meaning I mentioned Perry's
discussions with others about morality.  In particular I said that Perry
had a habit of asking how to define absolute morality.  Dave Burns asks:

> So why is it such a clever move to stump them with this definition
> question? .... Perry's opponents are entitled to ask him what
> he's got to offer that deals with their problem better than the
> language that they're using.

I fear I didn't make it clear that Perry did have a better (read:
clearer) definition to offer - namely that of the competing protection
agency approach to define PPL.  Each signs up the PA that totes their
favourite brand of morality and we see which type of agencies takes
which fraction of the market to guide our thoughts on public
morality/conduct.  If, say, 90% of the PA market says that insulting
someone's ancestors in public is an actionable offence then you'd be
wise to adjust your behaviour to follow suit.  If you don't then your
rates with your PA rise to cover the cost of paying off all the others.

Wrong behaviour is expensive!  A pretty useful definition.

> As you may have guessed, people who go around asking for definitions
> are a pet peeve of mine. 

It can be annoying, but (sometimes!) very informative if they have
something better to offer.

Hope that's helpful.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

PS thanks for the info about Polanyi.  Sounds like I agree with him.

