From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: Computing futures: the killer PDA

I have had the idea for a long time of writing an essay entitled "The
Virtues of the Trailing Edge." On the trailing edge of technology the
hardware is cheap and available, the software is debugged and much of it
free--and it does most of what you want. On the leading edge everything is
going to be ready real soon now, as my friend Jerry says.

The immediate reason for mentioning this is that I have a PDA--one that I
find extraordinarily useful, elegant, and adequate for everything I
actually want to do with it. It is a Psion 3a. While not literally on the
trailing edge--it only came out a few weeks ago, replacing my old
Psion3--it looks that way in comparison with the Newton et. al.

The Psion runs for months on two AA batteries. It fits easily in an inside
pocket of a sports jacket, and lumpily in a pants pocket. It has a
Macintosh style interface. Built-in software includes a fairly 
sophisticated word processor, including style sheets, on screen italics,
boldface, etc., a spread sheet, a (rather simple) data base, a very
sophisticated agenda application, its own programming language, ...  . It
connects at 19,200 baud over a serial cable to a Mac or PC, and the word
processor can save in rtf. The keyboard is small but well designed; I can
type at least as fast as I can write illegibly and a good deal faster than
I can write legibly--which means a lot faster than I could write for a
machine that depends on handwriting recognition. The screen is 480 pixels
by 160, and can represent clearly print as small as I want to use. Almost
all of the applications support four levels of zoom, so you can use tiny
print and see about as much text as you would on an old style PC, or big
print and read easily at a distance. It has 512K of RAM internally and can
take up to 4 Megs of flash RAM.

It is a thorougly satisfactory machine and I recommend it highly. If you
insist on gadgets that do silly but neat things, you can even use it to
digitize your voice to produce custom alarms for occasions of your choice.
One of these days I will have to take a look at a Newton--but I already
know it is too big. 

